Romhack Base: Unknown... Video Series?
Timeline Placement: A few generations after Skyward Sword
Length: Medium (Could fill the map of ALttP)
Significance: Proper placement for the first two Zelda games, and the series' namesake

Synopsis:
The game starts with a series of short scenes with narration, after the manner of the Prince's story as told in Zelda 2's player manual:
The King of Hyrule had the full Triforce, and Hyrule had prosperity under his reign. However, the King eventually died, and a funeral is held.
(The Princess may say something to the effect that she now fears the idea of falling asleep and never waking up...)
After the funeral, everyone believed the Prince would ascend the throne with the full Triforce as well...
...but at the Coronation, only the Triforce of Power is revealed to be left to him.
The King's old advisor (the Magician) instills the Prince with a fear of those who would overthrow him if he didn't get the full Triforce, and soon.
The Princess attempts to comfort the Prince, saying he doesn't need the full Triforce to be a good king, but her words don't reach him.
The Princess's caretaker Impa takes her to the North Castle while the Prince tears apart South Hyrule Castle searching for any clue towards the missing Triforce pieces...
[Gameplay begins.]
[Act 1: The Search]
In Hyrule Castle, the Prince gets a key to the Royal Crypt where his father was just buried.
The Royal Crypt (Dungeon 1) is shaped like the Hylian Crest room-wise, and is full of puzzles and traps. (There are no monsters in Hyrule at this time.)
In the Royal Crypt, he finds a Blue Candle that he can use to light the way and ignite torches.
The King's coffin does not hold the missing Triforce pieces, but does have a mural above it depicting his close relationship with Hyrule's Three Dragons...
The Prince heads east to Faron Woods where he speaks to the dragon Faron. She says she knows not where the missing pieces could be...
...but that he ought to use a key she gives him to access the Triforce's old resting ground beneath the Temple of Hylia.
The Prince uses the key in a room beneath the Statue of Hylia to enter the Ancient Keep.
The Ancient Keep (Dungeon 2) is basically a long-deactivated version of Skyward Sword's Sky Keep. Plants are dead. Lava is cold stone. Rooms do not rearrange.
The player drops into the sandy room through a skylight, and travels counter-clockwise from top-middle to true center, backtracking after getting the item to retrieve the boss key.
The item is the Prince's iconic Whip. There are some active robot foes, and the boss may be a damaged version of Dreadfuse. The boss room is the Sky Keep's starting room.
At the end, the Prince finds nothing. He returns to Faron, who simply suggests asking another dragon.
The Prince travels west from the Temple of Hylia to reach the dragon Lanayru. After aiding him with a task, Lanayru has an ancient robot give him a lift up to Skyloft's capital: the Palace of Winds.
The Prince of Hyrule is greeted warmly by Groose's descendant and current Emperor of the Skyloft nation, but he turns oddly cold when he learns of the Prince's search for Triforce parts.
The Prince searches the Palace of Winds (Dungeon 3) on his own. The Emperor uses his loftwing to catch him when he falls, but he gives no aid in the Prince's "rude probing of his palace halls."
The Prince obtains a Bomb Bag here, and faces a boss like Mazaal called Ultimate Groosinator.
After defeating it, the Emperor gets mad and practically kicks the Prince out before he can search the Monument of Groose (Groose's resting place, depicting him with a small bird on his finger).
The Prince returns to the surface with no further option but to go ask Eldin on Death Mountain. He does so, but no leads are to be offered from final dragon.
The Magician convinces the Prince to accompany him to visit a pair of witches on the mountain not too far away.
The twin hags say the truth is with the Princess of Hyrule, and they give the Magician an amulet to use to get her to reveal the truth.
The Prince and the Magician proceed to the north of Death Mountain to North Hyrule (Zeld 2's map), where they find that the Princess is not at the North Castle.
After a fight, one of the Princess's lingering entourage at the North Castle reveals she went to the Harbor Town of Mido.
Arriving at Mido, the Prince accosts his sister and tries to compel her to tell him what she knows about the missing Triforce parts.
She refuses to say, so the Magician uses the amulet the hags gave him to start a spell against her: a spell to make her fall into an endless sleep.
Shocked, the Prince commands the Magician to stop, fights him, begs him, but it's all of no use. The Magician casts the spell on the frightened but stalwart Princess.
The Princess collapses, and the Magician dies, leaving the Prince alone to grieve in the street.
[Act 2: The Remedy]
Full of rage, the Prince returns to the witches to either compel them to heal his sister, or to avenge her.
The witches say a cure is possible, but he must acquire some things first. They magically send him to the secret home of the Caretaker of Time: the Temple of Souls.
Inside the Temple of Souls, the Prince fights the Four Fates and retrieves the Forbidden Book, handing it over to the hag sisters. They congratulate him, and send him next to gather 3 special Flames...
The Prince travels deep into the western desert, in defiance of the local Zuna Tribe, to enter the Desert Colossus where the Blue Flame is said to reside.
The Desert Colossus (Dungeon 4) is the Spirit Temple before it was carved by the Gerudo. It contains the Boomerang.
On top of the desert mountain is a thin flat lake of water, a place closely reminiscent of Demise's Battlefield. There, the Prince obtains the first of the three flames.
The Prince then heads to the eastern ruins, where he is aided by a group of archaeologists. They help him to descend a deep pit to the mysterious Inverted Temple, where the Red Flame is hidden.
The Inverted Temple (Dungeon 5) is not much like any other zelda dungeon, but remains upsidedown the entire time. The item is the Cane of Pacci. The flame is in a deep dark cave, with red lava in the background.
After returning to the hags with the first two flames, they reveal the location of the final flame: a previously invisible tower just beside their location on Death Mountain.
The Tower of Hera (Dungeon 6) has the Hammer as its item. The flame at the end is not green, but purple. The Prince gets a bad feeling about it...
The witch sisters reveal themselves as Koume and Kotake, take the 3 Dark Flames and create monsters to plague the land once more.
They then beseige the Palace of Winds, destroy Groose's monument, where they find the Triforce of Wisdom hidden. They curse all loftwings to become tiny birds and lay waste to the Palace of Winds.
With Skyloft Nation destroyed, its people fleeing to the surface with what strength remains in their loftwings, and Hyrule attacked by a new wave of monsters, the Prince runs away in shame.
The Prince ends up on the northeastern isle, where he collapses to his knees on a cliff overlooking the sea, with a notion of jumping off. But before he does, he repents and asks for some miracle for his kingdom.
A magical Silver Sword is given to him (Zelda 2 title image), and he knows what he must do.
[Act 3: Reclaimation]
The Silver Sword has the ability to make its weilder stronger the more enemies it defeats.
The Prince travels to the south end of the great isle, through the Valley of Death, to the entrance of the Great Palace.
Koume and Kotake know the Triforce of Courage is within, and they attempt to break the King's Binding Force to enter. The Prince uses the Silver Sword to drive them away.
The Prince then travels all over Hyrule, defeating monsters and saving his people, unable to accept their gratitude for knowing his part in bringing these troubles about.
Desert Colossus 2: boss is Vitreous.
Inverted Temple 2: boss is Death Armos.
Tower of Hera 2: boss is Moldorm.
Spectacle Rock: Koume and Kotake hide within. The Prince obtains Silver Arrows and uses them to slay the witches and retake the Triforce of Wisdom.
The Skyloftians settle in the Fortress of Winds, and Hyrule celebrates peace returning to the land, but the Prince still mourns for his sister and all the pain his foolishness wrought...
[Act 4: Last Attempt]
A mysterious sheikah named Sheik (should show up earlier as well) tells the Prince that the Caretaker of Time can rewind time, undoing any mistake or failure.
The Prince once again invades the sacred Temple of Souls to compel the Caretaker to rewind time.
The Caretaker, Cia, insists that her duty forbids her from tampering with time in such a way. The Prince and her fight. Weakened, she creates a Dark Prince for the Prince to fight as well.
In the end, the Prince concedes defeat, and weeps. Then Sheik attacks Cia and steals her staff. Sheik reveals himself to be Ghirahim, and runs off to the Temple of Hylia to rewind time.
The Prince pursues, enters the Door of Time, and defeats the Magician and Ghirahim in an alternate timeline. There he meets another version of his sister and himself.
The Prince tells his other self to take good care of his sister and kingdom, warns him of a few things, and starts the rule that all Princesses of Hyrule thereforth should be named Zelda. He returns to his own time.
[The End]

